[Music]  i want pure colors melting clouds  accurately drawn details a sunburst  above a receding road with the light  reflected in furrows and ruts after rain  and no girls this was vladimir nabakov's  wish regarding the cover art for his  most famous and most controversial novel  the difficulty of publishing a book like  lolita not to mention the difficulty of  how to market and sell the novel has  been consistent since its first  publishing in 1955 the original  publishers olympia press discarded the  problem entirely by displaying nothing  pictorial at all  other editions of the novel took a  similar approach  however by 1962 matters changed as pop  culture started to consume and interpret  the novel  in new ways  stanley kubrick's movie of lolita marks  a clear change in the attitude of book  publishers  [Music]  how did they ever make a movie of lolita  turns out they  didn't though i don't want to give a  complete summary of either book or movie  1955's lolita is a disturbing and  deceptively written novel about a man  humbert humbert preying upon and  sexually abusing a 12 year old child  the movie on the other hand is played as  a dark comedy  bringing up lolita's age 216 and  the abuse is only vaguely implied this  disconnect between movie lolita and book  lolita is what fuels  popular consciousness and continues to  inform book marketing from 1962 forward  the promotional image of sue lyon and  the heart-shaped sunglasses taken by  photographer ben stern  is easily the most significant culprit  once this image became associated with  lolita it really didn't matter that it  was a terribly inaccurate portrait  it became the image of lolita and it was  ubiquitous  book publishing took a cue from the  movie after the success of the film  lolita had become a sex symbol at the  heart of all this  seems to be the desire to make the  sexual aspect of the novel more  palatable it's ironic that in attempting  to make aspects of the novel more  palatable film and book marketers were  overtly sexualizing  a child character designer peter  mendelson argues that in attempting to  sell a book  designers must not always but sometimes  pander to a public that on occasion  lacks the interpretive subtlety to parse  literary subtext  that is to say if the general reading  public expects a school  girl or a school girl uniform on a  lolita jacket  then book buyers and booksellers will  also be expecting a school girl or  school girl uniform on a lolita jacket  and one can then reasonably assume that  marketing departments and publishing  houses will want them as well  in the end going backward upriver  towards its source even editors begin to  take their cues from misinformed readers  at large  dieter e zimmer's online gallery  covering lolita where i found most of  these book covers  offers a fascinating view of lolita's  publishing history ending with  contemporary publications  still today designers must contend with  the question of how to depict a  character who was seen entirely through  the eyes of their abuser  in lolita the story of a cover girl  edited by john bertram and yuri leving  this issue is posed to a number of  artists  unrestrained by commercial demands the  designs confront the subject in a number  of ways  usually indirectly some artists focus on  objects relating to lolita  some focus on the language of the novel  however most never attempt to portray  the character in a direct way  for example this design plays with the  idea that lolita is  impossible to portray on a book cover  even the word is blurry and distant the  design that really stands out to me  however  is this  for years lolita has had her identity  stolen and transformed she has been the  lurid object on a book cover as seen  through the eyes of her abuser but in  this design what appears to be humbert  humber  displayed in his monstrous reality is  finally the subject  the name and persona of lolita is just a  manifestation of his sickness and we  finally see the true subject of the book  for whoever is responsible for marketing  and selling the novel publishing lolita  will always pose a problem  after all it's an incredibly nuanced  incredibly deceptive narrative and  popular culture has not treated lolita  the character  with much nuance in some ways it's  almost a curse that such a novel should  become so famous  and to become subject to such a  [Music]  misinterpretation

